# licences.tab -- WHAT EACH SHIPPED COMPONENT IS DISTRIBUTED UNDER, and what
#	         that obliges this project to do about it.
#
# A LICENCE OBLIGATION ATTACHES TO WHAT IS HANDED OVER, not to what the tree
# holds.  This tree holds material under twenty-odd sets of terms, and which of
# them anybody actually receives is decided by dist/lists/*.list: games/ttycity
# is GPLv3-plus-additional-terms and is built by nothing and staged by nothing,
# so it obliges us to nothing; net/screen is GPLv1 and ships setuid in `net', so
# it obliges us to source.  So this is a register of WHAT WE SHIP, keyed on the
# component, and it sits beside the lists that decide it.
#
#	dist/licences.tab		this file: the recorded judgement
#	dist/licences.py		collects the texts beside the release
#	dist/pack-component.sh		cuts the packages they travel with
#	LICENSE				the repository's own statement
#
# NOTHING HERE IS LEGAL ADVICE, and nothing here was decided by guessing.  Where
# a question could not be settled by reading the notices in the tree, the column
# says `?': a RECORDED judgement, "unsettled, and treated conservatively until a
# human settles it", which travels into the package as it stands.
#
# FORMAT.  Whitespace columns, `#' to end of line is a comment, two record
# kinds, so awk and sh can read this and no second parser is needed:
#
#   lic  <id> <src> <notice> <ship> <text> <marker> <what...>
#
#	<id>	 the licence regime, and the name of the collected text file
#	<src>	 must the corresponding source be shipped or offered?
#		 y | n | ?   `?' is treated as `y': the component must publish a
#		             -src package that cuts clean, and the question stays
#		             open.
#	<notice> must the notice travel with the BINARIES?
#		 y | n | ?   `?' is treated as `y'.
#	<ship>	 is our redistribution settled under these terms at all?
#		 y | ?       `?' is reported every run and never goes green
#		             silently.  It is not treated as `n', because the
#		             material is on an image today and pretending
#		             otherwise would be the more dishonest of the two.
#	<text>	 the licence text, a path relative to os/ resolved on
#		 $C900_OSPATH exactly as the source map's paths are, so the
#		 words that travel are the producer's own file and not a
#		 transcription of it.  `LICENSE' with no slash means THIS
#		 repository's own LICENSE, which every package already carries.
#		 `-' means no text was found -- which is itself a finding, and
#		 is only allowed where <ship> is `?'.
#	<marker> a regexp the text must contain, `.' standing for a space.  The
#		 path alone is not enough: os/-relative paths resolve against
#		 the first root of $C900_OSPATH that has them, three repositories
#		 have a LICENSE, and a gate that only checked existence would
#		 happily pack the wrong one.
#
#   use  <component> <id> [<why...>]
#
#	one component under one regime.  A component is a lists/*.list with a
#	`package' line (PACKAGE-FORMAT, LIST PACKAGES); the gate refuses one
#	with no row at all, and refuses a row naming a component that is not one.
#
# HOW THE ROWS WERE ARRIVED AT, so a reader can redo it rather than trust it:
# `component.py component <c> bin' names the files the component installs and
# `component.py component <c> src' names the sources they are cut from, and every
# row below is a regime one of those two sets touches.  Where the -src set
# reaches material the -bin set does not, the `why' says so -- that is the
# source map's object-search over-reach (PACKAGE-FORMAT, THE SOURCE MAP), it
# makes a -src package carry MORE than it owes, and it is recorded rather than
# trimmed because trimming a source package to a guess is the failure this
# whole mechanism exists to avoid.

# ---- THE REGIMES ----------------------------------------------------------

# The backbone.  Robert Swartz released the Mark Williams COHERENT source in
# 2015 under BSD 3-Clause; its second condition is why `notice' is `y' for
# nearly every component here -- a binary redistribution must reproduce the
# notice "in the documentation and/or other materials provided with the
# distribution", and the licences collected beside the release are those
# materials.  The
# per-file "may not be copied without permission" headers predate the release
# and are kept as found; they do not narrow it.
lic mwc-bsd3       n y y LICENSE                        Swartz          MWC COHERENT, BSD-3-Clause (Swartz, 2015)

# This repository's own new work, under the MIT licence its LICENSE states for
# code written for this project: the ZView window server, its driver, its
# desktop applications, the Vellum drawing suite, and the distribution's own
# scripts and /etc files, which are the whole of what login-text and
# login-graphical install.  MIT obliges no source and does oblige the notice --
# "the above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in
# all copies" -- which every package carries as its own LICENSE.
lic mit-oc         n y y LICENSE                        MIT.License     this repository's own work (ZView, Vellum, the distribution's /etc files), MIT

# GPL.  THE VERSION IS NOT BOILERPLATE AND WAS CHECKED PER TREE.  All three
# oblige source; two of them are version ONE, which is not what this project
# said about them until the files themselves were read.
lic gpl2-gzip      y y y tools/gzip/COPYING               Version.2       gzip, GPL v2 or later
lic gpl1-rcs       y y y tools/rcs/COPYING                Version.1       RCS 4.3, GPL v1 or later (19 files say "either version 1"; "version 2" is nowhere in the directory)
lic gpl1-screen    y y y net/screen/COPYING             Version.1       GNU screen, GPL v1 or later -- COPYING is the v1 text and all 11 sources say "either version 1"; the GPLv2 this project previously recorded was wrong

# Bellcore's grant for MGR.  It obliges no source and it does oblige the notice:
# "the copyright notice must be reproduced on copies".  That sentence is the
# reason the collected texts sit beside the binaries and not only in the source,
# where mgr/COPYRIGHT already travels with the code.
lic bellcore-mgr   n y y mgr/COPYRIGHT                  Bellcore        MGR, Bellcore 1988: not for sale for profit, notice must be reproduced on copies, credit to Bellcore

# Berkeley, in its three materially different forms.  They are separate rows
# because they are separate obligations, not separate years.
lic ucb-bsd3       n y y games/bsd/COPYING              Regents         Regents of the University of California, 3-clause
lic ucb-adv        n y y base/lib/libcurses/curses.h               Berkeley        Regents, with the ACKNOWLEDGEMENT requirement: documentation, advertising and other materials must acknowledge Berkeley
lic ucb-bsd4       n y y net/include/net/gen/netdb.h    advertising     Regents, 4-clause: all advertising materials must display the acknowledgement

# Everything else that ships, each with its own text.
lic minix-ph       n y y net/inet/COPYING               Prentice        Minix 2.0.4 TCP/IP stack; Prentice Hall BSD-3-form at the top of the donor tree, per-file Homburg authorship lines
lic tetris         n y y games/bsd/tetris/COPYRIGHT     monetary        tetris: no fee or other monetary gain, and the statement of authorship must be reproduced on all copies
lic atc-james      n y y games/bsd/atc/def.h            Ed.James        atc, Ed James / UC Berkeley 1987
lic ckermit        n y y comms/ckermit/COPYING          Columbia        C-Kermit, Trustees of Columbia University: not sold for profit, notice retained
lic zoo            n y y archive/zoo/COPYING            Dhesi           zoo 2.1, Dhesi 1991: copying for any purpose, commercial or not, notices preserved
lic lharc          n y y archive/lharc/COPYING          FREEWARE        C-LHarc, Tagawa: "This is FREEWARE"
lic zmodem         n y y comms/zmodem/COPYING           Satchell        rz/sz: Satchell's and Brown's grants; Forsberg's public-domain statement cited second-hand
lic less           n y y tools/less/version.c             Nudelman        less: free use and modification, notice intact, not sold for profit without the author's consent
lic patch          n y y tools/patch/patch.c              Larry.Wall      patch, Wall 1986: copy freely, do not make money off it, do not claim authorship
lic elvis          n y y base/cmd/elvis/COPYING              redistributable elvis 1.4, Kirkendall: freely redistributable in source or executable form, no restrictions stated
lic pico           n y y editors/pico/pico.h                Washington      pico, University of Washington: any purpose without fee, notice retained, name-use restriction
lic spencer        n y y base/lib/regexp/regexp.c   Toronto         Spencer's regexp, University of Toronto 1986
lic top            n y y base/cmd/top/COPYING                LeFebvre        top 3.3, LeFebvre: freely redistributable, "this entire comment MUST remain intact"
lic nawk           n y y base/cmd/nawk/awk.h                 Hirabayashi     nawk, Hirabayashi 1989: any purpose without fee, notice and disclaimer retained

# ---- REGIMES WITH AN UNSETTLED COLUMN -------------------------------------
# Each of these is on an image today.  Read the `what' before changing a `?'.

# UnZip 4.1 as held forbids commercial use outright and forbids redistributing a
# MODIFIED copy -- and unzip.c and unzip.h here ARE modified.  Info-ZIP's own
# COPYING.OLD records Smith saying in 1994 that he had no objection to Info-ZIP
# carrying his code and wanted it to stay freely distributable, while noting the
# commercial stipulation still binds a company.  That is a statement of intent
# recorded by a third party, not a grant, and it does not reach a modified copy.
# The settled answer is the port nobody has done: UnZip 5.41 and later is under
# the Info-ZIP licence, which permits both.  SHIP IS `?' FOR A HUMAN.
lic unzip          n y ? archive/unzip/COPYING          commercial      UnZip 4.1, Smith 1989: no commercial use, no modified redistribution -- and our copy is modified

# 25 of 25 rogue sources carry the same restriction, so its scope is not in
# doubt; what is in doubt is who granted it and on what basis, because no
# upstream distribution has been matched to this tree.
lic rogue          n y ? games/rogue/main.c               traded          rogue: "not to be traded, sold, or used for personal gain or profit", uniform across all 25 sources, upstream unidentified

# Two files inside the GPLv1 RCS tree that carry neither the GPL boilerplate
# the other nineteen carry nor any grant compatible with it: partime.c and
# maketime.c are Harrenstien's, "not to be sold, nor used in licensed software
# without permission of the author".  build-rcs.sh compiles both into every RCS
# binary this project ships.  Whether the FSF's distribution of RCS 4.3 settles
# that, and whether the GPL's own terms can be met over a file whose author
# restricted it, is a question about two documents and is not resolvable by
# reading this tree.  SHIP IS `?' FOR A HUMAN.
lic sri-harrenstien n y ? tools/rcs/partime.c             Harrenstien     RCS partime.c and maketime.c, Harrenstien/SRI 1980-81: not to be sold nor used in licensed software without permission -- inside a GPLv1 tree

# A public-domain claim in a comment is a claim and not a dedication
# instrument.  Recorded as found, which is all anybody here can do.
lic pd-emacs       n n ? editors/me/main.c                  public.domain   MicroEmacs lineage, "public domain" claimed in the files themselves

# The class that has no text at all.  It is not one licence and it is not
# grouped for convenience: it is the answer "we looked and there is nothing",
# and it is on this table so that the programs it covers cannot be read as
# covered by the row above them.  See `use' rows for which programs.
lic no-grant       n n ? -                              -               copyright asserted or nothing at all, and NO permission statement found in the tree

# ---- THE COMPONENTS -------------------------------------------------------

use archive        zoo
use archive        lharc
use archive        unzip
use archive        zmodem
use archive        ckermit
use archive        mwc-bsd3         csu and libc, which every component links

use base           mwc-bsd3
use base           ucb-adv          libcurses, linked by the shell and by make

use runtime        mwc-bsd3
use runtime        ucb-adv          /usr/bin/cut, /usr/bin/more, and libcurses/libterm which most of runtime links
use runtime        ucb-bsd3
use runtime        spencer          /usr/bin/more links usr.bin-3.x/regexp
use runtime        top              /bin/top
use runtime        nawk             /bin/awk and /bin/nawk
use runtime        elvis            /usr/bin/{vi,ex,elvis}
use runtime        no-grant         /bin/{enroll,xencode,xdecode} from cmd/knapsack, which carries no notice of any kind
use runtime        bellcore-mgr     -src only: the source map's object search reaches mgr/src, so runtime-src carries MGR source that no runtime binary contains

use cpm            mwc-bsd3
use cpm            ucb-adv          libcurses

use editors        pd-emacs         /usr/bin/me and /usr/bin/emacs
use editors        pico             /usr/bin/pico
use editors        mwc-bsd3
use editors        ucb-adv          libcurses

use games          ucb-bsd3
use games          ucb-bsd4         /usr/games/adventure carries the 4-clause form
use games          ucb-adv          libcurses
use games          atc-james        /usr/games/atc
use games          tetris           /usr/games/tetris
use games          rogue            /usr/games/rogue
use games          mwc-bsd3         /usr/games/fortune and its database are MWC's own
use net-games      ucb-bsd3         hunt and huntd, 4.4BSD games
use net-games      ucb-adv          libcurses, which hunt links
use games          gpl1-screen      -src only: object-search over-reach onto cmd/screen; no games binary is screen
use games          bellcore-mgr     -src only: object-search over-reach onto mgr/src

use hr             mit-oc           the ZView server, its driver, the desktop catalogs and the icons
use hr             mwc-bsd3         the rendering engine the server links, the 16 raw FM fonts the .hf faces are generated from (they carry no notice of their own and their origin is unestablished; they are byte-identical to the COHERENT 0.7.3 Z8001 holding, which is the material this row covers), csu and libc

use hr-clients     mit-oc
use hr-clients     mwc-bsd3         csu, libc, libm and the rendering engine every client links

use hostfs         mwc-bsd3

use login-graphical mit-oc           /etc/ttys.mgr and /etc/rc.local
use login-text     mit-oc           /etc/ttys


use mail-light     mwc-bsd3
use mail-light     minix-ph         smtpd and smtpsend come from the Minix networking lineage
use mail-light     ucb-adv          libcurses
use mail-light     gpl1-screen      -src only: object-search over-reach onto cmd/screen
use mail-light     bellcore-mgr     -src only: object-search over-reach onto mgr/src

use mgr            bellcore-mgr     the server, the fonts, the icons and the menus
use mgr            mwc-bsd3         csu and libc

use mgr-clients    bellcore-mgr
use mgr-clients    mwc-bsd3
use mgr-clients    ucb-adv          libcurses
use mgr-clients    no-grant         /usr/mgr/bin/{maze,ico}: an IBM 1985 block and a DEC/MIT-lineage client inside the Bellcore tree, neither carrying a grant of its own

use net            gpl1-screen      /usr/bin/screen, which ships setuid
use net            minix-ph         /etc/inet, the stack itself
use net            ucb-bsd4         /bin/rlogin and the resolver headers carry the 4-clause form
use net            ucb-adv          libcurses
use net            ucb-bsd3         /bin/finger, /bin/host
use net            mwc-bsd3
use net            bellcore-mgr     -src only: object-search over-reach onto mgr/src

use toolchain      mwc-bsd3         the Z8001 compiler, assembler, linker and libraries, all MWC-derived

use toolchain-1985 no-grant         the twelve June 1985 Mark Williams binaries the reference disk shipped with -- cc, ccx, db, l, nld, cpp, cc0, cc1, cc2, cc3, scrts0.o, slibc.a -- for which no repository here holds source or terms.  Predating the 2015 COHERENT source release, so mwc-bsd3 does not reach them; this is why the component publishes no `src' kind.

use tools          gpl2-gzip        /usr/bin/{gzip,gunzip,gzcat}
use tools          gpl1-rcs         /usr/bin/{ci,co,rcs,rlog,ident,rcsdiff,rcsclean,rcsmerge,merge}
use tools          sri-harrenstien  partime.c and maketime.c, compiled into every RCS binary by build-rcs.sh
use tools          less             /usr/bin/less and /usr/bin/lesskey
use tools          patch            /usr/bin/patch
use tools          spencer          usr.bin-3.x/regexp, linked by less
use tools          ucb-adv          libcurses
use tools          mwc-bsd3

use vellum         mit-oc           the suite, its library and the stencil and example drawings
use vellum         mwc-bsd3         csu and libc
